  "methods_english": "The NFA comparison used the frozen AnchorBoost recipe and the published NeuroTrajectory conditional neural process at commit f9848800dfab66a8bc005e6b3087153eeaabe9ac. The primary cohort contained 194 source chemicals in folds 1-4; 49 fold-0 development chemicals were excluded. Both methods received the same five published three-concentration contexts per chemical and predicted the remaining concentrations. The endpoint was the mean absolute error against observed concentration-level means across four developmental days and 17 features, averaged equally over designs and chemicals. Original Linux AnchorBoost errors and published CNP errors define the primary table. Saved AnchorBoost predictions and the published three-seed CNP checkpoints provide all pointwise predictions. Paired percentile intervals used 10,000 chemical resamples with seed 0; 95% and 97.5% intervals are reported. A predeclared identity sensitivity omitted phenobarbital because its sodium form occurred in the training set, leaving 193 test identities.",
  "results_english": "AnchorBoost reduced NFA curve MAE by 2.79% relative to the published conditional neural process (1.160612 versus 1.193954; K minus C -0.033342, 95% CI [-0.070284, -0.000407]; n=194). It had lower error in 3/4 folds and 105/194 chemicals. The 193-identity sensitivity gave a difference of -0.029874 (95% CI [-0.065825, 0.002217]).",
  "methods_footnote": "The underlying platform is a static 48-well rat cortical-neuron MEA assay. Repeated designs and output cells share the same chemical. Phenobarbital and its sodium form share a parent identity across training and test folds. C checkpoint replay uses the public float16 response bundle and a CPU runtime; its largest per-chemical MAE difference from the original published table is 0.000198948. The original published errors define the primary comparison, and the replay errors are retained separately.",
